<Article h1={"12-week dead hang plan."} subtitle={"Organize your sessions around a repeatable baseline. This planning framework suits you once you can hold a comfortable 30 seconds or longer. It offers added load or one-arm work as options. It is not a promise to reach a particular hold time."} crumbs={[{"name":"Training plans","href":"/training-programs/"},{"name":"12 weeks"}]} updated={"Updated October 30, 2026"} title="12-Week Dead Hang Program: Plan, Log & Review" description="A free 12-week dead hang planning framework with review points, editable timer settings and a local training log. No guaranteed results or paid signup." schema={`<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"12-Week Dead Hang Program: Plan, Log & Review","description":"A free 12-week dead hang planning framework with review points, editable timer settings and a local training log. No guaranteed results or paid signup.","dateModified":"2026-10-30","mainEntityOfPage":"https://deadhangs.com/12-week-program/","author":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"}}</script>`}>
<div class="article-body"><h2>Before the first session</h2><p>Use a securely fitted bar and a stable way to step down. Choose an assisted or unassisted hold you can control without pain. Keep that variation consistent when comparing results. If you have an injury or concerns about the movement, seek individual guidance.</p><h2>Your 12-week framework</h2>{blocks.map(([week,title,body])=><section class="plan-option"><p class="dh-eyebrow">{week}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></section>)}<h2 id="session-structure">Plan around the rest of your training</h2><p>Choose repeatable practice slots that fit your existing routine. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free. Account for other grip work such as climbing or pulling exercises when deciding whether another hanging session is manageable.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Example guidance, common training practice and not a tested prescription. Adjust it from your own baseline.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Example 12-week guidance" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Weeks</th><th scope="col">Sessions per week</th><th scope="col">Sets and holds</th><th scope="col">Rest between sets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 to 4</td><td>3</td><td>3 sets at a hold you can repeat cleanly</td><td>60 to 90 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>5 to 8</td><td>3</td><td>Add about 5 seconds, or one set, once every set is clean</td><td>60 to 90 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>9 to 12</td><td>3</td><td>Retest, then repeat the setup that worked</td><td>60 to 90 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li>Write a starting setup for block one: bar, grip, support, hold interval, rest and sets.</li><li>Keep that setup stable enough to collect comparable sessions. Record what you actually complete.</li><li>Review at weeks four and eight. Keep a useful setup or change one variable for the next block.</li><li>Compare the last block with the first, using the same bar, grip and assistance. Export the record at week twelve before starting a new planning window.</li></ol><p><strong>Example decision:</strong> you complete more of your planned sessions in block two while your best hold stays at 30 seconds. Your consistency has improved even though your recorded maximum has not. A longer planning window does not require adding weight or moving to a one-arm hang. Each of those options has its own prerequisite.</p><h2>Choose your timer settings</h2><p>Enter your own hang duration, rest duration and number of sets. The timer on the training log page shows example defaults you can change. You can shorten a hold or increase rest before starting. The timer pauses when the page is hidden and never saves a session automatically.</p><a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Open the timer & log</a><h2>Record the work you actually did</h2><p>Save the date, assistance, best actual hold and number of sets. Compare supported hangs with supported hangs and unassisted hangs with unassisted hangs. Export the log before clearing your browser or switching devices.</p><h2>When the plan needs changing</h2><p>Stop a painful hold. End a set before you lose the ability to step down under control. A missed session does not require making up extra sets. Repeating a block is an option; longer holds are not the only reason to keep a training record.</p><h2>Review the block</h2><p>Compare your current hold with an earlier hold made under the same conditions. The comparison with your own earlier hold is the number that matters for this plan.</p><p>The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal calculator</a> shows the difference without inventing a population percentile. The printable session planner on the training plans page suits you if you prefer paper. Dead hangs are one part of a wider routine; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">NHS activity guidance</a> covers broader strength and aerobic activity.</p></div>
